 "post": {
  "title": "What 120 Hz really means",
  "caption": "What does 120 Hz on a phone really mean? 📱\n\nHertz = new pictures your screen draws each second. At 120 Hz each picture stays up about 8 ms (1000 ÷ 120), at 60 Hz about 17 ms, so a scroll moves in smaller jumps and looks smoother.\nThe surprise: adaptive screens slow right down when nothing moves. Apple's iPhone 14 Pro added a 1 Hz refresh for its always-on lock screen to save battery.\n\nOn a spec sheet, look for \"adaptive\" refresh: smooth when you scroll, easy on battery when you don't. Send this to the friend comparing phones.\n\nPhoto: see sources.",
